<p>A problem from the <a href="https://onedrive.live.com/?redeem=aHR0cHM6Ly8xZHJ2Lm1zL2YvcyFBcHZHbTRNZ1FCT19nUkp1QjJRSnFSdzlIMGxyP2U9dHloU1hF&#038;cid=BF134020839BC69B&#038;id=BF134020839BC69B%21162&#038;parId=BF134020839BC69B%21147&#038;o=OneUp">October 1961 issue</a> of <em>Eureka</em>, the journal of the Cambridge University Mathematical Society:</p>
<p>When A was three times as old as B was the year before A was a half of B&#8217;s present age, B was 3 years younger than A was when B was two thirds of A&#8217;s present age. A&#8217;s and B&#8217;s ages now total 73. How old are A and B?</p>

